WEBVTT

start-0
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.667
What does Finpoint help me do?. The problem. A bank description tells you money moved, but not what it was really for. A supermarket payment might include groceries and a gift. A dinner payment might include your friends’ shares.

start-1
00:00:13.667 --> 00:00:28.500
How Finpoint helps. Finpoint connects bank activity to your own explanations, then uses those explanations to show spending and planning. Start with one purchase. You do not need to organize every Category or set every target first.

start-2
00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:32.967
Step 1. Try the sample lunch lesson in Help & guides.

start-3
00:00:32.967 --> 00:00:39.200
Step 2. Import a supported bank CSV, or restore a Finpoint backup from Sync.

start-4
00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:44.100
Step 3. Explain one Statement with a Record and an Allocation.

start-5
00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:49.067
Step 4. Check your result, then request a backup from Sync.

start-6
00:00:49.067 --> 00:00:54.900
Check your result. You can explain where one bank amount went and find the Record again.

start-7
00:00:54.900 --> 00:01:03.467
If something looks wrong. An empty Dashboard is expected before you have Records. Importing Statements alone does not explain spending.

start-8
00:01:03.467 --> 00:01:16.967
Example. Alex paid $12 for lunch. The bank amount is −$12. The explanation is a Lunch Record, also −$12. An Allocation of −$12 connects them.

model-0
00:01:16.967 --> 00:01:25.967
What are Statements, Records, and Allocations?. The problem. Bank rows and real-life purchases do not always match one for one.

model-1
00:01:25.967 --> 00:01:43.567
How Finpoint helps. A Statement is one account activity entry. A Record is your explanation of an activity. An Allocation assigns an amount from a Statement to a Record. An Account identifies the financial source, not your login. Money paid is negative; money received is positive.

model-2
00:01:43.567 --> 00:01:47.567
Step 1. Read the Statement amount and description.

model-3
00:01:47.567 --> 00:01:51.800
Step 2. Choose what that activity means in real life.

model-4
00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:56.100
Step 3. Create or select the Record that explains it.

model-5
00:01:56.100 --> 00:02:04.933
Step 4. Allocate the appropriate signed amount. A Statement can feed several Records, and a Record can combine several Statements.

model-6
00:02:04.933 --> 00:02:12.367
Check your result. A complete Record has at least one Allocation, and its Allocation total equals its amount.

model-7
00:02:12.367 --> 00:02:22.167
If something looks wrong. Do not create a second explanation just because there are two bank rows. A payment and repayment may explain one shared purchase.

model-8
00:02:22.167 --> 00:02:35.667
Example. Statement −$12 → Allocation −$12 → Lunch Record −$12. An Account balance and the Dashboard spending total answer different questions.

workspace-0
00:02:35.667 --> 00:02:45.433
How do I start or restore a workspace?. The problem. A new workspace has starter Categories and spending buckets, but no personal bank activity.

workspace-1
00:02:45.433 --> 00:02:59.467
How Finpoint helps. Finpoint stores financial data in this browser on this device. The starter Categories and buckets are editable planning defaults. You can learn using the isolated practice lessons before importing your own data.

workspace-2
00:02:59.467 --> 00:03:04.700
Step 1. For a new workspace, open Importer and choose your bank.

workspace-3
00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:16.133
Step 2. If you already have a Finpoint JSON backup, open Sync and under Manual sync, choose your JSON file with Choose file, then use Restore from file.

workspace-4
00:03:16.133 --> 00:03:26.233
Step 3. Before restoring, export the current workspace if you need to keep it. Restore replaces financial data; it does not merge two workspaces.

workspace-5
00:03:26.233 --> 00:03:32.333
Step 4. Use Help in the top bar whenever you need an explanation without leaving a form.

workspace-6
00:03:32.333 --> 00:03:39.167
Check your result. Your expected Accounts, Statements, or restored Records appear in their lists.

workspace-7
00:03:39.167 --> 00:03:51.367
If something looks wrong. Do not use Load demo data as a safe tutorial in your own workspace: it replaces financial data. Help & guides practice is separate and uses sample values only.

workspace-8
00:03:51.367 --> 00:03:58.933
Example. A beginner can practice a −$12 lunch without uploading files or changing an Account balance.

import-0
00:03:58.933 --> 00:04:08.100
How do I import bank activity?. The problem. A bank file has to match a supported format before Finpoint can turn its rows into Statements.

import-1
00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:28.967
How Finpoint helps. Importer supports DBS, OCBC, UOB, and Revolut CSV exports. Importing loads Statements, not finished Records. Missing Accounts can be created during import. Revolut asks for Account details and accepts one file; the other supported importers allow multiple files.

import-2
00:04:28.967 --> 00:04:35.167
Step 1. Export a CSV from your supported bank. Keep its original columns.

import-3
00:04:35.167 --> 00:04:43.700
Step 2. Open Importer, select the bank, and select the file or files. For Revolut, choose or create the Account.

import-4
00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:50.133
Step 3. Submit the import and read the inserted, re-indexed, and unchanged counts.

import-5
00:04:50.133 --> 00:04:58.667
Step 4. Open Statements to check dates, amounts, and Account. Then open Allocator to explain the remaining amounts.

import-6
00:04:58.667 --> 00:05:04.333
Check your result. The expected bank rows are visible with the correct signs and dates.

import-7
00:05:04.333 --> 00:05:19.433
If something looks wrong. If import fails, check the selected bank and original CSV format. A PDF or renamed spreadsheet is not the same format. If no rows are inserted, inspect skipped counts and filters before importing repeatedly.

import-8
00:05:19.433 --> 00:05:32.167
Example. Alex imports a bank CSV containing a salary of +$3,000 and a lunch payment of −$12. Neither has a personal explanation until Alex creates Records.

lunch-0
00:05:32.167 --> 00:05:42.133
How do I explain my first purchase?. The problem. The imported lunch Statement says −$12, but the Dashboard needs the Record that explains it.

lunch-1
00:05:42.133 --> 00:05:57.167
How Finpoint helps. Allocator shows Statements with amounts still available to allocate. Create a Record from the relevant Statement, give it a useful title, date, amount, and Category, and check the proposed Allocation before saving.

lunch-2
00:05:57.167 --> 00:06:01.500
Step 1. Open Allocator and choose the lunch Statement.

lunch-3
00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:11.533
Step 2. Create a Record titled Lunch. Use Paid $12, represented as −$12, and select the appropriate food Category.

lunch-4
00:06:11.533 --> 00:06:19.567
Step 3. Check that the Allocation from the lunch Statement is −$12. Review treatment and bucket defaults.

lunch-5
00:06:19.567 --> 00:06:27.567
Step 4. Save, reopen the Record, and confirm its amount and Allocation total both equal −$12.

lunch-6
00:06:27.567 --> 00:06:34.400
Check your result. The Record is complete and the fully allocated lunch Statement leaves the remaining-amount list.

lunch-7
00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:45.067
If something looks wrong. If the Record is Pending, compare its amount with the saved Allocation total. Correct the amount or missing Allocation based on what actually happened.

lunch-8
00:06:45.067 --> 00:06:58.867
Example. −$12 bank activity → −$12 allocated → −$12 Lunch. Statement remaining: $0. Record difference: $0.

backup-first-0
00:06:58.867 --> 00:07:08.767
How do I protect my first results?. The problem. Automatically saved browser data can still be lost if you clear browser storage or change devices.

backup-first-1
00:07:08.767 --> 00:07:24.633
How Finpoint helps. Saving a Record writes to this browser. A Finpoint JSON backup gives you a separate copy of financial data and settings. Requesting a download cannot prove that the file was kept successfully, so check your Downloads folder yourself.

backup-first-2
00:07:24.633 --> 00:07:30.167
Step 1. Open Sync and under Manual sync, choose Download backup.

backup-first-3
00:07:30.167 --> 00:07:36.800
Step 2. Find the downloaded Finpoint JSON file and keep it somewhere you can find again.

backup-first-4
00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:42.833
Step 3. Keep an earlier backup before using Restore from file or replacing data.

backup-first-5
00:07:42.833 --> 00:07:49.200
Step 4. If you choose Google Drive sync, review its status and any conflicts separately.

backup-first-6
00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:56.433
Check your result. You have located the downloaded backup file; a button click alone does not confirm that.

backup-first-7
00:07:56.433 --> 00:08:08.100
If something looks wrong. If the browser blocks a download, allow it and request the export again. Never clear storage until you have a usable backup or have confirmed the intended synced copy.

backup-first-8
00:08:08.100 --> 00:08:19.167
Example. After explaining Lunch, Alex exports a backup. Later, Restore from file can restore that financial workspace, replacing whatever is currently there.

split-0
00:08:19.167 --> 00:08:33.333
How do I split one payment into different purchases?. The problem. One −$80 supermarket Statement includes $60 of groceries and a $20 gift. Putting everything in one Category hides the distinction.

split-1
00:08:33.333 --> 00:08:47.367
How Finpoint helps. Create two Records and allocate part of the same Statement to each. Allocations keep the bank link while Records explain the separate purposes. Their total should use the available Statement amount without exceeding it.

split-2
00:08:47.367 --> 00:08:55.533
Step 1. Create Groceries for −$60 and allocate −$60 from the −$80 Statement.

split-3
00:08:55.533 --> 00:09:04.033
Step 2. The Statement has −$20 left to allocate. Create Gift for −$20 and allocate that remainder.

split-4
00:09:04.033 --> 00:09:09.000
Step 3. Choose the correct Category and treatment for each Record.

split-5
00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:14.700
Step 4. Check both Records and confirm the Statement has $0 remaining.

split-6
00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:23.967
Check your result. −$60 plus −$20 equals −$80. Both Records tally and the Statement is fully allocated.

split-7
00:09:23.967 --> 00:09:35.133
If something looks wrong. An Allocation larger than the available amount is rejected. Reduce it or remove an incorrect existing Allocation; do not invent extra bank activity.

split-8
00:09:35.133 --> 00:09:46.500
Example. One Statement −$80 → Groceries −$60 and Gift −$20. You keep one bank row and two useful explanations.

repayment-0
00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:59.267
How do I record a shared dinner and repayment?. The problem. Alex pays $90 for dinner and later receives $60 from friends. Alex’s own cost is $30, not $90.

repayment-1
00:09:59.267 --> 00:10:13.233
How Finpoint helps. One shared-dinner Record can combine the payment and repayment Statements. Record the amount you are explaining after the repayment. Money received back for the same dinner does not have to become a separate Income Record.

repayment-2
00:10:13.233 --> 00:10:19.900
Step 1. Create Shared dinner with amount −$30 and an appropriate spending Category.

repayment-3
00:10:19.900 --> 00:10:25.100
Step 2. Allocate −$90 from the dinner payment Statement.

repayment-4
00:10:25.100 --> 00:10:31.900
Step 3. Allocate +$60 from the friends’ repayment Statement to the same Record.

repayment-5
00:10:31.900 --> 00:10:37.367
Step 4. Save and check that the Allocation total is −$30.

repayment-6
00:10:37.367 --> 00:10:46.033
Check your result. −$90 + $60 = −$30. Both Statements are explained and the Record tallies.

repayment-7
00:10:46.033 --> 00:10:59.300
If something looks wrong. Until the repayment is available, the Record may be Pending. Pending is an amount-matching status; it does not by itself prove that someone owes you money. Use your notes to explain the situation.

repayment-8
00:10:59.300 --> 00:11:10.467
Example. Payment −$90 → Shared dinner ← Repayment +$60. The Record shows Alex’s −$30 share.

treatments-0
00:11:10.467 --> 00:11:21.933
Are refunds, transfers, and savings spending?. The problem. The sign of money movement alone does not tell you whether it is income, spending, savings, or a transfer.

treatments-1
00:11:21.933 --> 00:11:38.533
How Finpoint helps. Treatment tells the Dashboard how to count a Record. Income, Spending, Saving/investment, and Transfer/neutral have different meanings. Automatic follows the amount direction. A Category can supply the default, and a Record can override it.

treatments-2
00:11:38.533 --> 00:11:44.400
Step 1. Use Income for salary, and Spending for purchases and their refunds.

treatments-3
00:11:44.400 --> 00:11:56.267
Step 2. For a transfer between your own Accounts, combine −$200 and +$200 into a $0 Record with Allocations; use Transfer/neutral.

treatments-4
00:11:56.267 --> 00:12:01.267
Step 3. Use Saving/investment for contributions and withdrawals.

treatments-5
00:12:01.267 --> 00:12:07.633
Step 4. Review the effective treatment on the Record rather than guessing from its Category name.

treatments-6
00:12:07.633 --> 00:12:19.667
Check your result. A +$20 Spending refund reduces net spending. Saving contributions display as positive magnitudes; net saving movement uses the cash perspective.

treatments-7
00:12:19.667 --> 00:12:33.300
If something looks wrong. A −$250 savings net means more cash went out to savings than came back. It is not evidence of a portfolio loss. A $0 Record without any Allocations is still Pending.

treatments-8
00:12:33.300 --> 00:12:49.667
Example. Contribution −$300 and withdrawal +$50 produce contributions $300, withdrawals $50, and net −$250. Salary +$3,000 is separate income.

pending-0
00:12:49.667 --> 00:13:00.300
Why is my Record Pending, and how do I fix it?. The problem. Pending can refer to a Record that does not tally, or to a pending Statement. These are different checks.

pending-1
00:13:00.300 --> 00:13:15.067
How Finpoint helps. A Record is Pending when it has no Allocations or their total differs from its amount. A pending Statement is a placeholder or supported imported pending bank activity. A Record can tally while still being linked to a pending Statement.

pending-2
00:13:15.067 --> 00:13:21.800
Step 1. Open the Record and compare its amount, Allocation total, and Allocation count.

pending-3
00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:32.133
Step 2. For a −$10 Record with a −$12 Allocation, correct the Record to −$12 if the lunch really cost $12.

pending-4
00:13:32.133 --> 00:13:40.200
Step 3. For missing bank activity, keep a pending Statement only when it represents the actual expected activity.

pending-5
00:13:40.200 --> 00:13:49.600
Step 4. When the real bank row arrives, use the pending-Statement replacement flow and verify the resulting links instead of allocating it twice.

pending-6
00:13:49.600 --> 00:13:57.867
Check your result. At least one Allocation exists and its total matches the Record. Also review pending Statements separately.

pending-7
00:13:57.867 --> 00:14:11.633
If something looks wrong. Correct the facts, not just the status. A mismatch is a valid saved state, not a failed save. If the real amount differs from the placeholder, inspect the affected Record after replacement.

pending-8
00:14:11.633 --> 00:14:24.500
Example. Record −$10; Allocation −$12; difference $2. Change the Record to −$12 when that is the correct purchase amount, then save and check again.

find-0
00:14:24.500 --> 00:14:37.600
How do I find, edit, or remove a mistake?. The problem. A saved item may be outside your filters, fully allocated, or in a different month. An empty list does not always mean your data is gone.

find-1
00:14:37.600 --> 00:14:56.200
How Finpoint helps. Records, Statements, and Allocator show different things. Allocator focuses on remaining amounts. Use search, Account or Category filters, dates, and Pending filters to narrow the appropriate list. Amount filters use Paid or Received with a positive value.

find-2
00:14:56.200 --> 00:15:02.800
Step 1. Check the selected month and date range first. Clear restrictive filters when searching.

find-3
00:15:02.800 --> 00:15:08.800
Step 2. Look for a fully allocated bank row in Statements rather than only Allocator.

find-4
00:15:08.800 --> 00:15:18.000
Step 3. Open the Record or Statement detail and use its edit actions. Review linked Allocations before changing amounts or removing data.

find-5
00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:23.200
Step 4. Save and recheck both the Record total and the Statement remainder.

find-6
00:15:23.200 --> 00:15:30.100
Check your result. The corrected item appears in the intended date range and its links still explain the activity.

find-7
00:15:30.100 --> 00:15:43.600
If something looks wrong. Changing a title does not fix a wrong amount. Changing a Record amount may leave it Pending until the Allocations agree. Destructive removal should be deliberate; keep a backup before large corrections.

find-8
00:15:43.600 --> 00:15:53.033
Example. To find payments over $50, select Paid and enter 50. You do not need to type a negative sign into the amount filter.

categories-0
00:15:53.033 --> 00:16:04.400
What is the difference between Category, treatment, and bucket?. The problem. Three controls describe different aspects of the same Record. Mixing them up makes totals confusing.

categories-1
00:16:04.400 --> 00:16:23.633
How Finpoint helps. Category describes what a Record is for. Treatment determines how analytics count it. A spending bucket is an optional planning group. Starter buckets are Daily, Recurring, Irregular, and Travel. A Category can provide treatment and bucket defaults; a Record can use its own choices.

categories-2
00:16:23.633 --> 00:16:28.533
Step 1. Open Categories to create or edit the labels you need.

categories-3
00:16:28.533 --> 00:16:34.133
Step 2. Set a sensible treatment and optional default bucket on the Category.

categories-4
00:16:34.133 --> 00:16:41.733
Step 3. When editing a Record, check whether its treatment and bucket follow the Category or are manually selected.

categories-5
00:16:41.733 --> 00:16:50.300
Step 4. Use bucket management on the Dashboard to maintain planning groups. Archiving a bucket preserves existing Records.

categories-6
00:16:50.300 --> 00:16:58.900
Check your result. Lunch can be a food Category, Spending treatment, and Daily bucket. These are three complementary choices.

categories-7
00:16:58.900 --> 00:17:09.900
If something looks wrong. A renamed Category does not automatically mean every manually overridden Record now has its defaults. Inspect the affected Record’s actual choices.

categories-8
00:17:09.900 --> 00:17:19.467
Example. Category: food. Treatment: Spending. Bucket: Daily. A rent Record can also be Spending while belonging to Recurring.

targets-0
00:17:19.467 --> 00:17:29.267
How do monthly bucket targets work?. The problem. You need a monthly comparison for a spending group, without moving money or changing bank balances.

targets-1
00:17:29.267 --> 00:17:43.300
How Finpoint helps. A bucket target is a planning comparison. A default applies from the selected month onward. A target set for only one month overrides the default for that month. Targets do not reserve cash or block spending.

targets-2
00:17:43.300 --> 00:17:48.833
Step 1. Choose the correct month on the Dashboard before editing a bucket target.

targets-3
00:17:48.833 --> 00:17:54.133
Step 2. Choose whether the amount is for this month only or from this month onward.

targets-4
00:17:54.133 --> 00:17:59.233
Step 3. Review the bucket’s target and spending in the selected month.

targets-5
00:17:59.233 --> 00:18:06.833
Step 4. Use pace and projection as estimates, and check month coverage before comparing with earlier months.

targets-6
00:18:06.833 --> 00:18:16.767
Check your result. Daily spending $127 against a $500 target leaves $373 in the planning comparison.

targets-7
00:18:16.767 --> 00:18:28.100
If something looks wrong. If the target seems wrong, inspect a month-specific override and the effective month of the default. Removing an override allows the applicable default to show again.

targets-8
00:18:28.100 --> 00:18:41.133
Example. A $500 default beginning in October carries forward. A $600 October-only target takes precedence in October, while later months use $500 unless changed.

budgets-0
00:18:41.133 --> 00:18:51.767
When should I use a Budget instead of a bucket?. The problem. A trip or event may span a custom period and several Categories, rather than a regular monthly spending group.

budgets-1
00:18:51.767 --> 00:19:07.133
How Finpoint helps. A Budget has its own amount, start and end dates, and attached Records. Its chart uses signed amounts from attached Records within the relevant date windows. This is a different view from the Dashboard’s treatment-based spending totals.

budgets-2
00:19:07.133 --> 00:19:12.233
Step 1. Open Budgets and create a Budget with an amount and date range.

budgets-3
00:19:12.233 --> 00:19:22.233
Step 2. Choose automatic attachment if you want all existing Records in that range attached at creation, and newly created Records attached when they qualify.

budgets-4
00:19:22.233 --> 00:19:29.067
Step 3. Review the actual attached Records; manually attach or remove Records when needed.

budgets-5
00:19:29.067 --> 00:19:38.567
Step 4. Check membership again after editing dates, Records, or the Budget. Updates do not automatically rebuild existing membership.

budgets-6
00:19:38.567 --> 00:19:45.200
Check your result. The intended event Records are attached and fall inside the period used by the chart.

budgets-7
00:19:45.200 --> 00:19:57.300
If something looks wrong. An unexpected Budget total may come from a missing attachment, an out-of-window date, or signed income or transfer amounts. It need not equal the Dashboard Spending number.

budgets-8
00:19:57.300 --> 00:20:05.767
Example. Use a Budget for a week-long holiday with selected Records. Use the Travel bucket for the broader monthly planning view.

dashboard-0
00:20:05.767 --> 00:20:18.267
How do I read the Dashboard and Monthly Records?. The problem. Useful charts depend on the month, coverage, Record dates, and treatments. Bank balances are not the same as these totals.

dashboard-1
00:20:18.267 --> 00:20:39.067
How Finpoint helps. The Dashboard summarizes Records by effective treatment, shows spending by Category and bucket, and provides pace and comparisons. Monthly Records lets you inspect the Records behind the selected month. Pending Records can still contribute to analytics, so review unresolved explanations before relying on totals.

dashboard-2
00:20:39.067 --> 00:20:44.267
Step 1. Choose the intended month and inspect its coverage information.

dashboard-3
00:20:44.267 --> 00:20:52.800
Step 2. Read Income, gross spending, refunds, net spending, surplus, and saving movements with their labels.

dashboard-4
00:20:52.800 --> 00:21:02.167
Step 3. Use Monthly Records to trace an unexpected amount back to its Record, Category, treatment, date, and Allocations.

dashboard-5
00:21:02.167 --> 00:21:11.000
Step 4. Review history comparisons and future Records in context; partial months and projections are not completed outcomes.

dashboard-6
00:21:11.000 --> 00:21:28.567
Check your result. With income $3,000, gross spending $147, and refunds $20, net spending is $127. Surplus is $2,873, or about 95.8% of income.

dashboard-7
00:21:28.567 --> 00:21:43.033
If something looks wrong. If a total is surprising, first check the selected month and the actual Record treatment. Then inspect Pending Records and coverage. A chart is a summary of the workspace, not a bank reconciliation guarantee.

dashboard-8
00:21:43.033 --> 00:21:52.767
Example. Saving net −$250 is displayed separately. It does not mean income fell or investments lost $250.

sync-0
00:21:52.767 --> 00:22:03.100
How do backups and Google Drive sync work?. The problem. You want a recoverable copy or another device to share the workspace, and need to know which copy is current.

sync-1
00:22:03.100 --> 00:22:25.000
How Finpoint helps. Financial data is stored locally. JSON export is a separate backup. Optional Google Drive sync shares a workspace copy through your connected Drive. Finpoint’s authentication broker handles sign-in, not your financial table contents. Learning progress stays on this browser and is excluded from financial backups and Drive sync.

sync-2
00:22:25.000 --> 00:22:31.067
Step 1. Open Sync, export a backup, and locate the downloaded file.

sync-3
00:22:31.067 --> 00:22:37.167
Step 2. Connect Drive only if you want sync, then read the status after changes.

sync-4
00:22:37.167 --> 00:22:45.567
Step 3. For Offline, reconnect and let the status update. For authentication errors, reconnect Google Drive.

sync-5
00:22:45.567 --> 00:22:56.167
Step 4. For a conflict, export the current local copy first, inspect the local and remote choices, then explicitly choose the copy you intend to keep.

sync-6
00:22:56.167 --> 00:23:04.167
Check your result. A confirmed Up to date status and a located backup answer different questions. Keep both when appropriate.

sync-7
00:23:04.167 --> 00:23:18.733
If something looks wrong. Do not repeatedly overwrite a conflict just to remove the warning. Restore from file and Load demo data replace financial data. A stale tutorial milestone cannot prove your current financial workspace is complete.

sync-8
00:23:18.733 --> 00:23:28.900
Example. A practice lesson can remember its completion on this browser without adding sample Records to your financial backup or uploading practice values to Drive.

routine-0
00:23:28.900 --> 00:23:38.167
What should I do each time I return?. The problem. A repeatable short routine is easier than trying to perfect the entire workspace in one sitting.

routine-1
00:23:38.167 --> 00:23:55.100
How Finpoint helps. Import new activity, explain remaining amounts, inspect Pending items, review the month, and protect the result. You can return to any video chapter, search the Help articles, or replay a practice lesson without restarting all onboarding.

routine-2
00:23:55.100 --> 00:24:00.800
Step 1. Import your latest supported bank CSV and check the result counts.

routine-3
00:24:00.800 --> 00:24:09.533
Step 2. Use Allocator for unexplained amounts; use Records to review incomplete explanations and pending Statements separately.

routine-4
00:24:09.533 --> 00:24:19.333
Step 3. Review the correct month in Dashboard and Monthly Records. Check treatments, buckets, targets, and Budget membership when relevant.

routine-5
00:24:19.333 --> 00:24:28.067
Step 4. Check Sync and keep backups. Settings lets you adjust default date filters and comparison history to suit your routine.

routine-6
00:24:28.067 --> 00:24:36.100
Check your result. You can follow the bank amount to the explanation, understand the summary, and recover your saved work.

routine-7
00:24:36.100 --> 00:24:49.433
